It is not approved by the Open Source Initiative and it does not meet the Open
Source Definition or the Free Software Definition. Please do not describe this
project as open source, and please do not add it to a list of open source tools.

The source is published so that it can be read, audited, and understood —
particularly because this software performs security analysis, and software that
inspects your code should be inspectable in return. The licence grants reading.
It does not grant reuse.


IN PLAIN LANGUAGE
─────────────────

  You may, without asking:

    ✓  read, study, and learn from the source
    ✓  clone it so you can read it locally
    ✓  run it on your own machines, for your own work, including commercial work
    ✓  run it against systems you own or are authorised to test
    ✓  quote short excerpts in a review, a lesson, an article, or a security
       report, with attribution
    ✓  open an issue containing whatever excerpt is needed to describe a defect
    ✓  submit a fix

  You may not, without written permission:

    ✗  redistribute it, in source or compiled form
    ✗  publish a fork or a mirror
    ✗  modify it and distribute the result
    ✗  run it as a hosted or managed service for other people
    ✗  build it into a product or service you distribute or host
    ✗  remove the copyright or licence notices
    ✗  use the name or the logo to endorse or promote something else

  And two things worth knowing before you rely on it:

    !  This software can be wrong in both directions. It may report a defect
       that does not exist, and it may stay silent about one that does. No output
       from it is a statement that anything is secure.
    !  Point it only at systems you own or have documented permission to test.
       Anything else is outside the permissions granted here and is your
       responsibility alone.

The summary above is written to be read. Where it and the terms below could be
read differently, the terms below govern.


